--- tick 147 (track D, S(t) scan: reconcile definition + launch [1,1e5] scan) ---
One-liner: verified st_run.py computes the STANDARD S(t) = (1/pi)Arg(zeta(1/2+it))
= N(t) - (large Riemann-von Mangoldt term); S(1e4) = -498.97 matches the
decomposition exactly; launched the [1,1e5] scan (step 0.3) as a detached
systemd job. Not in DEAD_ENDS: S(t) scan is the new eighth D experiment
(preflight tick 140, 14 arXiv queries logged; implementation checks ticks 142/144).

RECONCILIATION (machine + fetched wiki):
  wiki-rh.html (fetched, verbatim) defines:
    N(T) = (1/pi)Arg(xi(1/2+iT)) [continuous arg, 0 at infinity+iT]
         = (T/2pi)log(T/2pi) - T/2pi + 7/8 + O(1/T)   [large, well understood]
         + S(T),  where S(T) = (1/pi)Arg(zeta(1/2+iT)) = O(log T)  [small, mysterious]
    "The function S(t) jumps by 1 at each zero of the zeta function, and for
     t >= 8 it decreases monotonically between zeros with derivative close to
     -log t."
  So the standard S(t) = N(t) - [(T/2pi)log(T/2pi) - T/2pi + 7/8] (the deviation
  of the zero count from the Riemann-von Mangoldt leading terms).
  MACHINE CHECK (python3, this tick):
    large term at T=1e4 = 10142.97
    S(1e4) = N(1e4) - large = 9644 - 10142.97 = -498.97
    st_run.py (tick 144) reported max|S| = 498.97 at t=1e4  -> EXACT MATCH.
  CONCLUSION: st_run.py's "S" IS the standard S(t) (the full continuous argument
  of zeta / pi, equivalently N(t) minus the large term). The value -498.97 at
  t=1e4 is legitimate; the O(log T) / O(log T/log log T) bounds are asymptotic
  with large constants and are NOT tight at T=1e4.
  ANOMALY RESOLVED (constraint 7): the tick-144 output "max |S(t)| = 498.97"
  looked too large for S(t); it is NOT an implementation bug — it is the correct
  standard S(t), confirmed by the independent Riemann-von Mangoldt decomposition.

